1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,600
In this video we'll take a look at how to manage the BurpSuite Enterprise Edition services.

2
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:17,700
Services will be installed for the Enterprise server, the web server, the scanning machines,

3
00:00:17,700 --> 00:00:24,060
and the database if you are using the bundled database rather than your own external one.

4
00:00:24,060 --> 00:00:28,860
You might want to check if these services are running or manually stop and start them.

5
00:00:28,860 --> 00:00:33,660
The process varies slightly depending on your operating system.

6
00:00:33,660 --> 00:00:37,460
The first step is to list your services.

7
00:00:37,460 --> 00:00:47,460
On Linux you can do this using the command sudo systemctl and grepping the output to show the services that contain Burp.

8
00:00:47,460 --> 00:00:58,460
On Mac else you would use the command sudo launchctllist and again grep the output to show the services that contain Burp.

9
00:00:58,460 --> 00:01:09,060
On Windows you can use the built-in services tool which can be found by going to the control panel System and Security Services.

10
00:01:09,060 --> 00:01:14,260
You can then use the UI to find the relevant services in the list.

11
00:01:14,260 --> 00:01:18,260
The status column will show if the service is currently running.

12
00:01:18,260 --> 00:01:23,060
To stop a running service again this will change depending on your OS.

13
00:01:23,060 --> 00:01:30,860
On Linux you can use sudo systemctl stop followed by the service name.

14
00:01:30,860 --> 00:01:42,260
On Mac OS systemctl would be replaced by launchctl so sudo launchctl stop followed by the service name.

15
00:01:42,260 --> 00:01:52,860
On Windows once you have found the appropriate service under the control panel System and Security Services.

16
00:01:52,860 --> 00:01:59,260
You can use the stop button within the dialog to stop the service or right click the service and select stop.

17
00:01:59,260 --> 00:02:12,860
Starting a service that is currently stopped follows a similar pattern but replace the stop with start.

18
00:02:12,860 --> 00:02:19,860
So on Linux you can use the command sudo systemctl start followed by the service name.

19
00:02:19,860 --> 00:02:27,460
Again on Mac OS sudo launchctl start followed by the service name.

20
00:02:27,460 --> 00:02:31,460
And on Windows in the services dialog use the start button.

21
